# Never expose secret values

Secret values must never enter the conversation, tool output, or any file I write.
This includes API keys, tokens, passwords, connection strings, and private keys.

## Never do this

- Read or open secret-bearing files to inspect their values: `.env`, `.env.*`, `*.pem`,
  `*.key`, `credentials*`, `*service-account*.json`.
- Print a secret with `cat`, `type`, `Get-Content`, `echo`, `printenv`, `env`, `$env:NAME`,
  or by embedding it in a command line.
- Output a secret in any derived form: masked, truncated, first/last characters,
  base64, or hashed. A partial value is still a leak.
- Copy a secret from one file or command into another.

## Diagnose with derived facts instead

When debugging a credential problem (401, "invalid api key", missing config), report only
facts computed in a script that never prints the value:

- whether the variable is set, and its character length
- which file or scope it came from (`.env`, shell/session, Windows User or Machine scope)
- boolean comparisons, e.g. "the shell value differs from the `.env` value"
- formatting flags: surrounding quotes, whitespace, `\r`, BOM in the file

```js
// GOOD - emits facts, never the value
console.log({ set: value != null, length: value?.length, matchesEnvFile: value === fromEnvFile });

// BAD - leaks the value
console.log("key:", value);
console.log("key prefix:", value.slice(0, 12));
```

## When a secret must change

Describe the exact edit for the user to make themselves: which file, which variable,
which scope to clear. Never read the old value and never write the new one.

If a tool result unexpectedly contains a secret, stop, say so, and advise rotating it.

`scripts/env-doctor.mjs` in this repo follows these rules and is the safe way to inspect
credential configuration here.
